{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure opsadmin on node1 for SSH access using the exact supplied public key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The opsadmin account exists with the expected home and shell. Its authorized_keys content exactly matches the supplied ED25519 key, is owned by opsadmin with mode 0600 and an SSH-compatible SELinux context, and persisted after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-89b7c2c2a15643cf","cmd-50f3ce46ab1e47c5","cmd-af109d3c233d4431"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Require key-based SSH authentication.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The effective sshd policy enables public-key authentication, disables password and keyboard-interactive authentication, and requires AuthenticationMethods publickey. Live unauthenticated SSH attempts were rejected while advertising only publickey, both before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2799786b174641ed","cmd-721f576eda9e4f0e","cmd-af109d3c233d4431","cmd-231aae3824a04218"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Account/key creation and password locking were required by the access outcome (cmd-89b7c2c2a15643cf); the sshd policy file and reload implemented key-only authentication (cmd-2799786b174641ed); and the scheduled reboot was required by the provider-wide restart protocol (cmd-bbb89873673e4bb6). The failed probe was the expected connection interruption during reboot and shows no unrelated mutation. Afterward there were no failed services, no unexpected listening ports, no damaged package state, and only normal reboot-generated systemd temporary-directory turnover.","evidence_ids":["cmd-89b7c2c2a15643cf","cmd-2799786b174641ed","cmd-bbb89873673e4bb6","cmd-cf06d3048d08419e","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both material outcomes are satisfied. The mandatory restart protocol is complete and coherent: node1 was healthy before restart, the restart was acknowledged and observed through connection loss, the boot ID changed from a0d04bc6-5f90-4c7b-8283-51dd6932056f to dff20be0-4b08-4224-bd8b-1429447f658c, and sshd, the account, exact key, permissions, and key-only policy recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently confirm the boot-ID transition.","limitations":["No successful SSH login using the corresponding private key was captured. Functional readiness is nevertheless strongly supported by the exact authorized_keys content, valid ownership/mode/SELinux context, healthy listening sshd, effective publickey-only policy, and live SSH negotiation that offered only publickey authentication."]}
